          %h2#definition Definition
          %p Classically, we have:
          .num_defn
            %p
              A
              %strong Grothendieck topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              is a category that admits a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} geometric embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi lex
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{T} \stackrel{\stackrel{lex}{\leftarrow}}{\hookrightarrow}
                    PSh(C)
            %p
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
              category, i.e., a full and faithful functor that has a left exact left adjoint.
            %p
              This is equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"}> Set
              \-valued
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
              satisfying the sheaf condition) over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+toposes+are+equivalently+the+left+exact+reflective+subcategories+of+presheaf+toposes"} sheaf toposes are equivalently the left exact reflective subcategories of presheaf toposes
            for discussion of this characterization.
          %p Since smallness can be relative, we also have:
          .num_defn
            %p
              For a given fixed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} category of sets
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %strong Grothendieck topos
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              \-valued
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
              satisfying the sheaf condition) over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
              relative to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            Note that a Grothendieck topos is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            because (or if)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            is not considered part of the structure; different sites may give rise to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            category of sheaves.
          %p
            By the general theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaves
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} geometric embedding
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi PSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(S) \hookrightarrow PSh(S)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                This may be taken as an alternative definition of
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topologies
                are bijectively given by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embeddings"} geometric embeddings
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            %li
              %p
                For details on the relation between the two perspectives see
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} geometric embedding
            %li
              %p
                This perspective is useful for defining the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
                of sheaves:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"} stacks
                and
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-sheaves
            %li
              %p
                Sometimes it is useful to distinguish between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/petit+topos"} petit topos
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros topos
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#general General
          #LocallyPresentable.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Every Grothendieck topos is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Borceux94"}> Borceux 94, vol 3, prop. 3.4.16
            )
          #total.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Every Grothendieck topos is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+category"} total category
              and a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cototal+category"} cototal category
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              From the page
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+category"} total category
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"}> Cocomplete
                ,
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-powered+category"} Well-copowered
                (because
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
                objects of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                are in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
                on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small set
                of these because a Grothendieck topos is well-powered), and
              %li
                Has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
                (e.g., the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
                of all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representables
                for a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+site"} small site
                presentation).
            %p Dually, a Grothendieck topos is
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"}> Complete
                ,
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-powered+category"} Well-powered
                (because
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
                of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                are in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
                with elements of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Ω
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(X, \Omega)
              %li
                Has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cogenerator"} cogenerator
                (e.g., the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+objects"} power objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^c
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                ranges over representables for a small site presentation).
            %p Therefore a Grothendieck topos is also cototal.
          %h3#Giraud Giraud's axiomatic characterization
          %p
            Giraud characterized Grothendieck toposes as categories satisfying certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exactness+property"} exactness
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} completeness
            properties (where “small” is again relative to the given category of sets
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+theorem"} Giraud's theorem
            characterises a Grothendieck topos as follows:
          %ol
            %li
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small"} locally small
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              with a small
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generating+set"} generating set
            %li
              with all finite
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            %li
              with all small
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+coproduct"} disjoint
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+stability"} pullback-stable
            %li
              where all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/congruences"} congruences
              have effective
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+objects"} quotient objects
          %p These conditions are equivalent to
          %ul
            %li
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small"} locally small
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+pretopos"} infinitary pretopos
              with a small generating set.
          %p
            See the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
            = succeed "-pretopos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-powered+category"} well-poweredness
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universes"} Grothendieck universes
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\in V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_V
            satisfies all the other conditions relative to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Set
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_U
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://secure.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/wiki/Topos#Giraud.27s_axioms"} Wikipedia
          %p
            Sometimes (3,4) are combined and strengthened to the statement that the category has all small
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
          %h3#Streetcharacterization Street’s axiomatic characterization
          %p
            Augmenting the aforementioned Proposition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#total"}
            that Grothendieck toposes are total categories,
            %a{:href => "#Street"} Street
            more or less characterizes Grothendieck toposes as
            %em lex total
            categories having the same “size” as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            In more detail: we take as background set theory ZFC + “there is a strongly inaccessible cardinal”
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            (a set of “small sets”). Supposing given a model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                    %mo +
                    %mi universe
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC+universe
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            be the category of small sets; note that the set of morphisms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            has size
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            Recall that locally small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lex+total+category"} lex total
            if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y: E \to [E^{op}, Set]
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact+functor"} left exact
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Street)
              A category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is a Grothendieck topos iff it is lex total and the size of the set of isomorphism classes of objects is
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
              or less.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This result is in the spirit of saying “every Grothendieck topos is the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
              with respect to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+topology"} canonical topology
              on itself”. Putting aside set-theoretic issues, it suggests that Grothendieck toposes be seen as analogous to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
              between them would be analogous to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
              between them.
            %p
              One can deduce formally that lex total categories are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+pretoposes"} Heyting pretoposes
          %h3#as_lex_reflections_of_categories_of_presheaves As lex reflections of categories of presheaves
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+toposes+are+equivalently+the+left+exact+reflective+subcategories+of+presheaf+toposes"} sheaf toposes are equivalently the left exact reflective subcategories of presheaf toposes
          %h3#AsLocalicGroupoids As localic groupoids
          %p
            Every Grothendieck topos is equivalent to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+of+a+localic+groupoid"} classifying topos of a localic groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos"} with enough points
            is equivalent to the classifying topos of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groupoid"} topological groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
            of Grothendieck toposes is equivalent to a localization of that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+groupoids"} localic groupoids
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+of+a+localic+groupoid"} classifying topos of a localic groupoid
            for more.
          %h3#internal_logic Internal logic
          %p
            Being an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            that can be taken to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order logic
            or a form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"} geometric logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            and because every geometric theory has a Grothendieck
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
          %p
            Although the internal logic of a Grothendieck topos is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+logic"} constructive logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            is
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            way”. These include:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WISC"}> WISC
              \: every set has a weakly initial set of covers.
            %li
              The (constructive)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+multiple+choice"} axiom of multiple choice
              (AMC).
            %li
              The
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+stack+completions"} axiom of stack completions
            %li
              (allegedly) the local
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+cardinality+selection+axiom"} small cardinality selection axiom
              (SCSA).
            %li
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+small+category"} complete small category
              is a preorder.
          %h3#2monadicity_over_locally_presentable_categories 2-monadicity over locally presentable categories
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of Grothendieck toposes and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+morphisms"} logical morphisms
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monadic
            over the 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocontinuous+functors"} cocontinuous functors
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BungeCarboni1995"} Bunge and Carboni 1995
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+topos"} symmetric topos
            for more details.
          %h3#enrichment_over_accessible_categories_with_directed_colimits Enrichment over accessible categories with directed colimits
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of Grothendieck toposes and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+bicategory"} enriched as a bicategory
            over the 2-category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+categories"} accessible categories
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+colimits"} directed colimits
            (note that accessible categories always have
            = succeed "-filtered" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            = succeed "-accessible)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+colimits"} directed colimits
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DiLiberti22"} Di Liberti 2022
          %h2#in_weak_foundations In weak foundations
          %p We have two definitions of a Grothendieck topos:
          %ul
            %li the category of sheaves on some small site,
            %li a category that satisfies Giraud's axioms (as listed above).
          %p
            The theorem that these are equivalent can be proved in quite weak foundations, whether
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitist+mathematics"} finitist
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            (or all three at once), as long as we axiomatize correctly given the caveats listed in the previous section. Some hard-nosed predicativists (and even hard-nosed ZFC fundamentalists) may object to the language (on the ground that large categories such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            and other nontrivial Grothendieck toposes don't really exist), but they should accept the theorems when suitably phrased.
          %p
            In predicative mathematics, however, we cannot prove that every Grothendieck topos is in fact a
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the+category+of+sets"} the category of sets
            is a Grothendieck topos, but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is an elementary topos if and only if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            are small, which is precisely what predicativists doubt. One can use the term
            %strong Grothendieck pretopos
            to avoid implying that we have an elementary topos. On the other hand, since Grothendeick toposes came first, perhaps it is the definition of ‘elementary topos’ that is too strong.
          %p
            Similarly, in finitist mathematics, we cannot prove that every Grothendieck topos has a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
            and small
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
          %p
            Constructivism as such is irrelevant; even in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+topos"} boolean
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            (and hence any category of sheaves contained within it) is boolean. (Again,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            itself is an example of this.)
          %p
            The theorem that every Grothendieck topos is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            is a subtle point; it fails only in finitist predicative mathematics. (The key point in the proof is to generate the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitive closure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim^*
            of a binary relation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msup
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo *
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim^* b
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∼
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∼
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∼
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim x_0 \sim \cdots \sim x_{n-1} \sim b
            for some
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msup
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo *
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim^* b
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∼
                  %mo ′
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \sim' b
            for every transitive relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim'
            that contains
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
          %h2#examples_and_nonexamples Examples and non-examples
          %p
            By definition, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            is a Grothendieck topos. Examples include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
                category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi PSh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PSh(C) = Set^{C^{op}}
                is a Grothendieck topos.
            %li
              %p
                In particular
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                is a Grothendieck topos, because it is equivalent to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi PSh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PSh(\ast)
            %li
              %p
                Broadly, most of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
                that people have worked with are Grothendieck.
          %p
            On the other hand, there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos#ElementaryTopos"} (elementary) toposes
            that fail to be Grothendieck toposes:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Because a Grothendieck topos
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} has all small limits
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinSet
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
                is not Grothendieck.
            %li
              %p
                More generally, the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_{\lt \pi}
                of sets with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
                below any bound
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is not a Grothendieck topos, even when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+limit+cardinal"} strong limit cardinal
                so that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_{\lt \pi}
                is an (elementary) topos.
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, any category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi FinSet
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet^{C^{op}}
                of
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi FinSet
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi π
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_{\lt \pi}^{C^{op}}
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+limit+cardinal"} strong limit cardinal
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+topos"} effective topos
            %li
              %p
                Other non-Grothendieck toposes occur in the topos-theoretic approach to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
