          %h2#History History
          %p
            From
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"} Colin McLarty
            (sent to the
            %a{:href => "https://www.mta.ca/~cat-dist/"} Categories mailing list
            on Apr 14, 2018):
          %blockquote
            %p
              At the start of 1958
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
              believed the correct
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weil+cohomology"} Weil cohomology
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              would be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} derived functor cohomology
              of some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab(S)
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+of+abelian+groups"} sheaves of Abelian groups
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              —and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab(S)
              was likely to be the category of all Abelian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+objects"} group objects
              in some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(S)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/espace+etale"} espace etale
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
              and it should have some good exactness properties.
            %p
              On April 21, 1958 Grothendieck went to the Seminaire Chevalley to hear
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre"} Serre
              describe isotrivial fiber bundles on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety"} variety
              V, which are bundles that become trivial when pulled back to some surjective family of finite, unramified maps to V. Serre showed this gave the expected one-dimensional Weil cohomology groups of V. Grothendieck immediately told Serre it would work in all dimensions–which Serre found “very optimistic” at the time.
            %p All of this is well documented in familiar sources.
            %p
              Grothendieck’s 1973 topos lectures in Buffalo show that during Serre’s talk Grothendieck saw the Weil espaces etales over a scheme
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              should be patched together from Serre’s unramified maps to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(S)
              of sheaves of sets on S should be generated by the unramified maps to S, and should have arbitrary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              of these, and finite limits. He first sought to axiomatize this roughly the way his
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tohoku"} Tohoku
              paper had axiomatized sheaves of groups.
            %p
              A few changes came soon: He realized finite unramified maps should be replaced by flat unramified maps (just as topological espaces etales are trivial locally in the fiber and not on the base). He abandoned the axiomatic approach to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(S)
              as too vague and shifted to construction by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sites"} sites
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/espaces+etales"} espaces etales
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/espace+etale"} espace etale
              much beyond the topological case.
            %p
              But April 21 1958 was the birth of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              came later. (Lots of people are not named on the day they are born.)
            %p
              As of the summer of 1973 Grothendieck’s stated preferred definition of topos was still: a category with arbitrary
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud+axioms"} Giraud axioms
            %p
              Also in 1973 Grothendieck says the objects in any topos should be seen as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/espaces+etales"} espaces etales
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              of the topos, in a generalized sense that includes saying any orbit of a group action lies “etale” over a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA4"} SGA4
              Grothendieck had already given evidence that it would work for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/petit+topos"} petit topos
              but not
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gros+topos"} gros topos
          %h2#references References
          %p Basic exposition:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"} Colin McLarty
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Wells"} Charles Wells
              = succeed "," do
                %em Variable set theory
              %em Scientific American
              but unpublished (~1985) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www.math.mcgill.ca/barr/papers/vst.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BarrMcLartyWells-VariableSetTheory.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p A quick introduction of the basic facts of sheaf-topos theory is chapter I, “Background in topos theory” in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              %em Classifying Spaces and Classifying Topoi
              Lecture Notes in Mathematics 1616, Springer (1995)
          %p Textbook accounts include
          %ul
            %li#Borceux94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , vol 3 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
          %p Grothendieck topoi appear around section III,4 there. A proof of Giraud’s theorem is in appendix A.
          %p
            The proof of Giraud’s theorem for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topoi"} (∞,1)-topoi
            is section 6.1.5 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
          %p Street’s characterization of Grothendieck toposes is given in
          %ul
            %li#Street
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Notions of topos
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://journals.cambridge.org/download.php?file=%2FBAZ%2FBAZ23_02%2FS000497270000705Xa.pdf&code=c6248e4fa6a517c9bb451e4f5abcf862"} pdf link
          %p These are also called Giraud frames in
          %ul
            %li#Vickers22
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Vickers"}> Steven Vickers
              ,
              %em Generalized point-free spaces, pointwise
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.01113"}> arXiv:2206.01113
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p On 2-monadicity of the opposite of the 2-category of Grothendieck toposes:
          %ul
            %li#BungeCarboni1995
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marta+Bunge"}> Marta Bunge
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aurelio+Carboni"} Aurelio Carboni
              %em The Symmetric Topos
              , JPAA
              %strong 105
              (1995) pp.233-249.
          %p On enrichment of the 2-category of Grothendieck toposes:
          %ul
            %li#DiLiberti2022
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Di+Liberti"}> Ivan Di Liberti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em General facts on the Scott Adjunction
